      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The Very Large Telescope (VLT) is situated on Cerro Paranal, Chile at an altitude of about 2.6 km above sea level.",
      "evidence": "European Southern Observatory (ESO) site specifications.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The maximum line‑of‑sight distance from the VLT to the summit of Mount Everest, accounting for Earth curvature, is ≈ 517 km.",
      "evidence": "Geometric horizon formula d = √(2 R h₁) + √(2 R h₂) with Earth radius R≈6371 km, observer height h₁=2.6 km, target height h₂=8.848 km gives d≈182 km + 335 km ≈ 517 km.",
      "weight": 0.60,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The great‑circle distance between Paranal and Mount Everest is about 15,000 km, far exceeding the 517 km line‑of‑sight limit.",
      "evidence": "Haversine calculation using the coordinates of Paranal (24.6° S, 70.4° W) and Everest (27.99° N, 86.93° E).",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the mountain lies well beyond the geometric line‑of‑sight, the VLT cannot have a direct view of it.",
      "evidence": "If an object is farther than the horizon distance, Earth's curvature blocks any straight line between observer and object.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Even if line‑of‑sight existed, the VLT’s optics are optimized for astronomical (effectively infinite) distances and cannot be focused on a nearby terrestrial target without major re‑configuration.",
      "evidence": "Technical manuals state VLT instruments are designed for objects at astronomical distances; focusing on a few‑kilometre target would require redesign of the collimation and adaptive‑optics system.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [5]
    }
  ],
